@phdthesis{Kelly22,
  author  = {Kelly, Christy},
  title   = {Emergent geometry and discrete curvature in random graphs},
  school  = {Heriot-Watt University},
  year    = {2022},
  month   = {April},
  url     = {https://www.ros.hw.ac.uk/handle/10399/4591},
  note    = {**UPDATED 2026-09-21 (evening): the owner supplied the full text as `docs/reading/KellyC_0422_epsSS.pdf`, 347 pages; Secs. 4.3.1-4.3.2 and 4.5.2 read.** **The in-thesis evidence is more hedged than the abstract, and the exact words matter.** On 4-regular graphs (our case), Sec. 4.5.2: "the action minimising phase is not reached since the action does not vanish, perhaps suggesting that the system becomes arrested in some metastable state. This perspective is corroborated by the observation of small but apparently persistent hysteresis loops in the action. **This suggests that the phase transition is first-order, though in view of the smallness of the hysteresis loops it is not clear that this is indeed sufficient evidence to draw a definitive conclusion.**" Hysteresis loops are shown at N = 50, 100, 200, 350, 500 (Figs. 4.57-4.58). So the support for first order at degree 4 is *small persistent hysteresis at five sizes*, with the author declining to call it definitive. **Three further things worth having.** (1) **His key quantity is the Binder cumulant**, in the normalisation B = (1/3)(1 - <A^4>/<A^2>^2), which VANISHES for a continuous transition and is asymptotically non-zero for first order. Ours (Q15, the Challa-Landau-Binder form 1 - <H^4>/3<H^2>^2) tends to 2/3 and to less than 2/3 respectively; **the two differ by exactly 2/3 and are directly comparable** once that is subtracted. (2) **A warning that applies straight to our analysis:** the action spectrum is discrete, so "PA may take on the appearance of multiple superimposed Gaussians with this being a mere effect of discretisation". Our H = 16(N-S) + 4 lambda X is discrete too, so any two-peak detection must be checked against discretisation before it is believed. (3) **He doubts correlation lengths are even well defined here**: interactions are not short range, random regular graphs have diameters growing only logarithmically, and "we are thus faced with the very real possibility that correlation lengths are not well defined in combinatorial quantum gravity". That bears on any finite-size scaling, ours included. He also notes the residual non-vanishing action may be Brunekreef and Loll's "quantum flatness" or Trugenberger's entropic cosmological constant, and that the evidence does not presently decide between them. Original note follows. **ABSTRACT READ DIRECTLY (2026-09-21); full text NOT read -- the repository PDF link 404s and must be obtained another way. Read it before this entry is relied on for anything.** By the first author of KTB19. Three statements from the abstract, quoted exactly. (1) For cubic graphs: "numerical evidence suggests that the transition to this geometric phase is continuous". (2) **"Higher degree results are less conclusive and the phase transition appears to become first-order, but there are good analytical and numerical indications that configurations retain a nearly geometric phase."** (3) "The critical temperature appears to be asymptotically nonfinite". **Why this matters more than anything else found so far.** Sentence (2) is the combination VISION claim 4 needs -- first order AND a space -- stated by the group's own first author, for degrees above 3, which includes the degree-4 case this project simulates. It sits against [T25], which reports the transition as continuous, and beside [GV21], which finds first order for degree > 3 independently. Our own framing that "where it is sharp there is no space and where there is a space it is smooth" is a statement about the ground state at two settings of lambda, and this is evidence that it does not hold for the transition itself. **Caveats, all the author's own or ours:** "less conclusive"; "appears to"; "nearly geometric" is not geometric; we have read one paragraph. Unknown from the abstract: which degrees "higher degree" covers, which variant of the energy, and what sizes. Sentence (3) also bears on T9: a critical temperature that is not asymptotically finite is the same phenomenon as the drift of the crossover with ln N that we measure.}
}

